In 'The Heretic Sage', dukkha is discussed in this context: *"There are two; 
the first is  sukhan’ti pi vijānāti, dukkhan’ti pi vijānāti, 
adukkhamasukhan’ti pi vijānāti."* Here, dukkha (suffering (dukkha)) is treated not as a theoretical doctrine, but as a living faculty to be actively cultivated and observed. When we bring genuine mindfulness to our direct experience, the quality of dukkha helps dismantle habitual delusion and stabilizes the mind in clear, unentangled awareness.

In 'The Magic of the Mind', samādhi is discussed in this context: *"Whatever view such a one has, that becomes for him Right view;
whatever intention he has, that becomes for him Right Intention;
whatever effort he puts forth, that becomes for him Right Effort;
whatever mindfulness he has, that becomes for him Right
Mindfulness: and whatever concentration he has, that
becomes for him Right Concentration."* Here, samādhi (concentration (samādhi)) is treated not as a theoretical doctrine, but as a living faculty to be actively cultivated and observed. When we bring genuine mindfulness to our direct experience, the quality of samādhi helps dismantle habitual delusion and stabilizes the mind in clear, unentangled awareness.

In 'The Ballad of Liberation from the Khandhas', Dhamma is discussed in this context: *"[Section: The Ballad of Liberation]
I pay homage to the one Well-gone,
the Foremost Teacher, the Sakyan Sage,
the Rightly Self-Awakened One;
& to the nine transcendent Dhammas;
& to the Noble Saṅgha."* Here, Dhamma (the Dhamma — the truth, the teaching, the nature of reality) is treated not as a theoretical doctrine, but as a living faculty to be actively cultivated and observed. When we bring genuine mindfulness to our direct experience, the quality of Dhamma helps dismantle habitual delusion and stabilizes the mind in clear, unentangled awareness.

In 'The Ballad of Liberation from the Khandhas', the teaching opens with this observation: *"I pay homage to the one Well-gone,
the Foremost Teacher, the Sakyan Sage,
the Rightly Self-Awakened One;
& to the nine transcendent Dhammas;
& to the Noble Saṅgha."* The central invitation is to turn one's investigation inward — to verify the Dhamma not as an external philosophy, but as directly observable reality in one's own body, speech, and mind. As the text emphasizes, the Dhamma is *akāliko* (timeless) and *opanayiko* (leading inward), realizable (*paccattaṁ*) by each practitioner through patient, honest awareness.

The Twelve Modes represent the complete, three-fold cycle of insight applied to all Four Noble Truths:

1. Knowing the truth (Sacca-ñāṇa);
2. Knowing the required practice/duty (Kicca-ñāṇa);
3. Knowing the completion of the practice (Kata-ñāṇa).

When all twelve modes are directly experienced within your own heart, the Wheel of Truth has been fully turned, and supreme awakening is accomplished.

In daily practice, remember to anchor yourself in the seat of The One Who Knows (Buddho). Do not fight conditions with anger, and do not grasp after pleasant states with desire. Observe all sensations, emotions, and thoughts as fleeting, impermanent formations (anicca saṅkhāra) that have no permanent owner (anattā). When you let go of resistance and rest in spacious awareness, the heart naturally discovers the unshakeable peace of the Unconditioned (Asaṅkhata Dhamma).

The Four Right Exertions under Right Effort mean:

1. Preventing unarisen evil;
2. Abandoning arisen evil;
3. Cultivating unarisen goodness;
4. Perfecting arisen goodness.

You maintain steady, joyful vigilance, keeping your garden of mind free from weeds.

